1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,662
Add the odd numbers in order. One. One plus three is four.

2
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:08,886
Add five, and you get nine. Then sixteen, then twenty-five.

3
00:00:08,983 --> 00:00:12,826
Every sum is a square number. Five examples show a pattern.

4
00:00:12,926 --> 00:00:15,544
They do not prove a rule. A picture can.

5
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:20,052
Start with one square. Three squares wrap it into a two by two square.

6
00:00:20,152 --> 00:00:23,930
Five more make three by three. Seven make four by four.

7
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:25,466
Nine make five by five.

8
00:00:25,566 --> 00:00:27,310
Why does this always work?

9
00:00:27,410 --> 00:00:31,009
Each L has one new row, one new column, and one corner.

10
00:00:31,109 --> 00:00:34,687
For the fifth square, that is four, plus four, plus one: nine.

11
00:00:34,787 --> 00:00:38,621
In general, the n-th L has two n minus one squares.

12
00:00:38,721 --> 00:00:40,989
That is exactly the n-th odd number.

13
00:00:42,083 --> 00:00:45,874
Pause here and predict. What is the sum of the first ten odd numbers?

14
00:00:48,966 --> 00:00:53,078
Ten L shapes make a ten by ten square. So the sum is one hundred.

15
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:57,269
The rule holds for every n, because each step adds the same kind of L.
